    "witness": "Stephen MacKenna · 1926 first edition",
    "reference": "Enneads V.1.2",
    "language": "en",
    "excerpt": "Let every soul recall, then, at the outset the truth that soul is the author of all living things",
    "context": "Let every soul recall, then, at the outset the truth that soul is the author of all living things, that it has breathed the life into them all, whatever is nourished by earth and sea, all the creatures of the air, the divine stars in the sky; it is the maker of the sun; itself formed and ordered this vast heaven and conducts all that rhythmic motion: and it is a principle distinct from all these to which it gives law and movement and life, and it must of necessity be more honourable than they, for they gather or dissolve as soul brings them life or abandons them, but soul, since it never can abandon itself, is of eternal being.",
    "translator": "Stephen MacKenna · historical translation",
    "rights": "Public-domain historical translation",
    "rightsUrl": "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Index:Plotinus_-_The_Enneads_Vol._4_-_tr._Stephen_MacKenna_(1926).djvu",
